<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boxer Rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1901">1901</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Boxer Uprising, Yihetuan Movement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Boxer Rebellion was an anti-foreign and anti-Christian uprising in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> led by the Yihetuan, the 'Militia United in Righteousness', known in the West as the Boxers for their martial rituals. Spreading through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> and Zhili in 1899 and 1900, the Boxers killed missionaries and thousands of Chinese Christians, and in June 1900, after the Qing court under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a> declared war on the foreign powers, they besieged the foreign legations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. An international force of eight nations relieved the legations in August 1900 and occupied the capital, and the Boxer Protocol of 1901 imposed a huge indemnity on China.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boxer-rebellion.jpg" alt="Boxer Rebellion" width="330" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taku_Forts_Landing.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…suppression of the Hundred Days’ Reform in 1898, placing the reformist Guangxu Emperor under house arrest and executing six of his reformist allies, ended the most ambitious internal attempt at systemic renewal, and the disastrous <b>Boxer Uprising</b> of 1900 brought foreign occupation of Beijing and a crushing indemnity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The defeat discredited the policy of borrowing techniques alone and led reformers to demand changes in institutions, in the Hundred Days’ Reform of 1898 and, after the <b>Boxer Rebellion</b>, in the New Policies of the 1900s.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Outcomes</a></li></ul></section>
